<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	App Transport Security.

	This has to be a real file: `NSAppTransportSecurity` is a dictionary, and
	Xcode's `INFOPLIST_KEY_*` build settings can only set top-level scalars. The
	project carried `INFOPLIST_KEY_NSAppTransportSecurity_NSAllowsArbitraryLoads`
	for several releases and it produced no key at all — the shipped Info.plist
	had no ATS section, so the policy was whatever the default happened to be and
	the "Allow local HTTP" setting could not work on a device.

	`NSAllowsArbitraryLoads` stays false: every internet connection this app makes
	is HTTPS. `NSAllowsLocalNetworking` is what permits cleartext to a host on the
	local network, which is where a self-hosted TeslaMate usually lives — and it
	is scoped to exactly that, so it cannot be used to reach anything public.
	`ProfileDraft.isLocal` enforces the same boundary before a URL is ever saved.
	-->
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoads</key>
		<false/>
		<key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
</dict>
</plist>
